WEBVTT

00:01.433 --> 00:03.133
- Driving, we're talking
about driving in Seattle!

00:03.133 --> 00:05.467
(sings) Driving,
driving in Seattle!

00:05.467 --> 00:09.000
driving in Seattle, driving
in Seattle is great.

00:09.000 --> 00:10.333
No, it's not.

00:10.333 --> 00:13.933
(funky instrumental music)

00:20.200 --> 00:22.567
(laughs)

00:22.567 --> 00:26.600
(raps) Tiny bubbles in the
stream, nightmare or a dream,

00:26.600 --> 00:30.267
slow and safe, fast-paced,
I'm somewhere in between.

00:30.267 --> 00:33.767
Organized chaos,
riding on an asphalt.

00:33.767 --> 00:35.100
Might be your ass fault.

00:35.100 --> 00:36.700
Wish I could fast-forward.

00:36.700 --> 00:38.200
(music)

00:38.200 --> 00:39.667
I'm not going to rant
about driving in Seattle.

00:39.667 --> 00:41.000
I'm not.

00:41.000 --> 00:42.500
Well, maybe a little bit,

00:42.500 --> 00:44.333
and admit that I'm complicit
in some of these things

00:44.333 --> 00:45.633
that I'm gonna call out.

00:45.633 --> 00:48.567
Four-way stop culture
in Seattle, typically,

00:48.567 --> 00:51.300
four people staring
at each other,

00:51.300 --> 00:53.500
waiting for somebody else to go.

00:53.500 --> 00:55.800
Nobody knows who has
the right-of-way,

00:55.800 --> 01:00.633
and everybody getting clearly
frustrated at one another,

01:00.633 --> 01:04.400
or maybe themselves, that
nobody's taking the initiative.

01:04.400 --> 01:08.033
Merging culture in Seattle,
it's simple; right?

01:08.033 --> 01:11.000
It's one lane
merging into another.

01:11.000 --> 01:13.567
It's like a zipper, and
you take turns; right?

01:13.567 --> 01:15.500
And you've all had zippers
mess up on you; right?

01:15.500 --> 01:18.300
We all had zippers, when
you try to pull up a zipper,

01:18.300 --> 01:19.700
and you realize, oh,

01:19.700 --> 01:23.167
one thing didn't hook
right into the other.

01:23.167 --> 01:25.533
And that's almost like
driving in Seattle.

01:25.533 --> 01:28.433
It's like broken zippers
everywhere, all the time.

01:28.433 --> 01:32.800
And, God forbid, you honk at
someone who is taking up space,

01:32.800 --> 01:35.000
slowly, in the left lane.

01:35.000 --> 01:37.433
They'll look at you like
you're the crazy one.

01:37.433 --> 01:39.467
The honk is totally normal.

01:39.467 --> 01:41.300
It is a form of communication.

01:41.300 --> 01:43.867
It's nothing out of the norm.

01:43.867 --> 01:46.400
In a place like New York, L.A.

01:46.400 --> 01:50.733
or any large, third-world
country, metropolis

01:50.733 --> 01:54.300
the honk is like, "Hey"
or "Look over here"

01:55.733 --> 01:59.967
or "Warning, I'm going
through. Just watch out."

01:59.967 --> 02:02.100
But in Seattle, the
honk, for many years,

02:02.100 --> 02:07.100
has been like, "I hate you" or
"You're hurting my feelings."

02:08.467 --> 02:09.500
I went to Manila,

02:09.500 --> 02:10.933
and people were talking about

02:10.933 --> 02:13.567
how bad the traffic
was gonna be.

02:13.567 --> 02:15.400
And yeah, it was bad,

02:15.400 --> 02:19.400
but within that chaos was
this weird organizing.

02:20.433 --> 02:21.433
And over here, I feel like

02:21.433 --> 02:23.200
there isn't much
of an agreement.

02:23.200 --> 02:27.500
But there is a culture
of doing it your own way,

02:27.500 --> 02:29.833
which can lead to
inefficiencies.

02:29.833 --> 02:32.333
Driving is one of
those things where

02:32.333 --> 02:34.700
we're all in our
own little world,

02:34.700 --> 02:39.567
and it can be a world of
isolation or frustration.

02:39.567 --> 02:44.100
But I think, ultimately, the
car is the ultimate metaphor

02:44.100 --> 02:48.267
for where we are at in
society, both good and bad.

02:49.500 --> 02:51.967
It's an expression
of the individual;

02:51.967 --> 02:53.800
many individuals
trying to find a way

02:53.800 --> 02:55.800
to get from point A to point B,

02:55.800 --> 02:57.833
all the different ways
that we can do it,

02:57.833 --> 03:00.667
all the different ways it
can be organized chaos.

03:00.667 --> 03:02.300
It could resemble warfare.

03:02.300 --> 03:05.367
It could look very
smoothly, if we want it to.

03:05.367 --> 03:06.600
I don't know.

03:06.600 --> 03:08.633
Sometimes, I just
wanna go for a walk.

03:08.633 --> 03:11.133
(jazzy music)
